WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.500
Well, I have so many.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.230
I remember that
when I was a kid,

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.690
I used to go to the
movies with my mom

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.540
and watch up to four
movies in a row.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.200
Probably, it has to be
the very first Star Wars

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.370
movie that I saw.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.580
Everybody that knows me know
I love Independence Day.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.450
It was-- Mani Ratnam
who's a famous director,

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.160
his movie called Bombay.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:31.340
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.070
It was to see 101 Dalmatians.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.510
My cousin took me
to a matinee of E.T.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.600
Seeing The Sound
of Music when I was

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.340
11 years old for my birthday.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.260
There is no better
moment than Meg Ryan's

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.300
faking an orgasm scene.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.470
That just took my
imagination to new heights.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:57.420
Singing, the colors, the entire
screen just blew my mind.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.190
I'll never forget feeling
the larger-than-life wash

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.410
of storytelling take over me.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.690
It felt like an
all-you-can-eat buffet.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.920
My mom packed us little
Ziploc baggies of candy.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.670
They were like, we are
not buying anything

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.430
from the concession stand,
because it's too expensive.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:19.440
The first time I saw that movie
was actually the first time

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.780
I went on a date as a teenager.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.020
All the seats were taken,
except the front row.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.510
So, of course, the worst
seat in the house, and I

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.040
was just staring up.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.070
And my neck was killing
me, and I didn't care.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.980
I remember driving all the
way out to an art house cinema

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.660
so that my parents
could sneak me in

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.900
to watch Chen Kaige's
Farewell My Concubine--

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.170
Yes!

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.300
Yes!

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.110
Yes!

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.970
Which is totally an
inappropriate movie

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.230
for like a six-year-old.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:51.150
The entire theater
was in an uproar,

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:55.020
just convulsions of
laughter and screaming.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.390
It was the first time that
I watched a movie where

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.800
I actually cried, and I
actually went on the journey

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.120
as if I was there.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.110
And it was like the first time I
realized, oh, this is powerful.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:13.470
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:29.870
[SPEAKING KOREAN]

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.560
I had all the action figures.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.060
I had the whole
Millennium Falcon,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.480
and I would enjoy playing
with all my Star Wars

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:37.500
action figures.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.490
And little did I know, I
think, that doing so, it really

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.330
is the seeds to your own
storytelling ability.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:49.560
It was just like you wanted
to replay that scene again,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.910
right then in that moment, when
you were at the movie theater.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

